d, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y-2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta-2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta-4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/88/882f7487d40a724c331aebb3dd954b8beace88ff5a2e65b4b2b9abedd74885dc.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=0$ and $\\theta=\\pi.$\n\n![](images/41/410279d76b3d76135133c63fe19003839b783a5d4177aff6744bd950fcbe93bd.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,0\\le\\theta\\le \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{0}^{\\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["04b50b83-c4ee-50ea-ae8a-d5cc1f4efc8b"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "9f993769-4242-5c50-a578-e44a67fa0267" :answer/id #uuid "9f993769-4242-5c50-a578-e44a67fa0267" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4d85821c-4168-5a6a-994e-4c67538a143d" :answer/id #uuid "4d85821c-4168-5a6a-994e-4c67538a143d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3e0c7a54-ad42-5b46-9438-a5e86783d08d" :answer/id #uuid "3e0c7a54-ad42-5b46-9438-a5e86783d08d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{2}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "154fe4ca-a591-51af-b5a1-03adc5261ae6" :answer/id #uuid "154fe4ca-a591-51af-b5a1-03adc5261ae6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3cc7ec97-c47c-54d7-b497-f40e8148a957" :answer/id #uuid "3cc7ec97-c47c-54d7-b497-f40e8148a957" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "6397bb0a-9d78-551e-a3e8-a73e2230a72a" :answer-field/id #uuid "6397bb0a-9d78-551e-a3e8-a73e2230a72a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["9f993769-4242-5c50-a578-e44a67fa0267" "4d85821c-4168-5a6a-994e-4c67538a143d" "3e0c7a54-ad42-5b46-9438-a5e86783d08d" "154fe4ca-a591-51af-b5a1-03adc5261ae6" "3cc7ec97-c47c-54d7-b497-f40e8148a957"] :answer-field/correct "3cc7ec97-c47c-54d7-b497-f40e8148a957" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "fa53fde6-b454-5423-91b4-30fdb6c5d5b2" :question/id #uuid "fa53fde6-b454-5423-91b4-30fdb6c5d5b2" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}\\le 4\\},$ then $\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/57/57ffd44d5406aafa448571f9a56eebded9a9916def605c08eb4eb1ad1e0df56c.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $2$ centered at $(0,-2).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}=4$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x^{2}+(y+2)^{2}&=4\\\\(r\\cos\\theta)^2+(r\\sin\\theta+2)^2&=4\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta+r^2\\sin^2\\theta+4r\\sin\\theta+4&=4\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r^2+4r\\sin\\theta&=0\\\\r(r+4\\sin\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/0b/0b3138e7b6599e8174ccd46648b334b18a505757b845e53af59d0b98e3b4c92b.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=\\pi$ and $\\theta=2 \\pi.$\n\n![](images/11/11dcaf5545227d8231eef23ee29ce52723be103c8a209a578927cacb83d56384.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le - 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,\\pi\\le\\theta\\le 2 \\pi\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{\\pi}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{- 4 \\sin{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["6397bb0a-9d78-551e-a3e8-a73e2230a72a"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "3543a771-464b-542c-9335-538c3709149f" :answer/id #uuid "3543a771-464b-542c-9335-538c3709149f" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}1\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "ac2b1f00-489c-598a-af58-abeb3809e74d" :answer/id #uuid "ac2b1f00-489c-598a-af58-abeb3809e74d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{0}^{2 \\pi}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "86580f35-f153-55b3-b4bc-2e64e0c0b121" :answer/id #uuid "86580f35-f153-55b3-b4bc-2e64e0c0b121" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4528835f-33a6-5ec5-9a47-a775cb974623" :answer/id #uuid "4528835f-33a6-5ec5-9a47-a775cb974623" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{- 6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "487a6fa0-57bb-500c-9371-92da4be661ac" :answer/id #uuid "487a6fa0-57bb-500c-9371-92da4be661ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "980036b8-3088-5457-9263-9a3e9604d20c" :answer-field/id #uuid "980036b8-3088-5457-9263-9a3e9604d20c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["3543a771-464b-542c-9335-538c3709149f" "ac2b1f00-489c-598a-af58-abeb3809e74d" "86580f35-f153-55b3-b4bc-2e64e0c0b121" "4528835f-33a6-5ec5-9a47-a775cb974623" "487a6fa0-57bb-500c-9371-92da4be661ac"] :answer-field/correct "487a6fa0-57bb-500c-9371-92da4be661ac" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "b2b48fc3-a0dd-5bed-af7d-8c8aed9c00ae" :question/id #uuid "b2b48fc3-a0dd-5bed-af7d-8c8aed9c00ae" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}\\le 9\\},$ then $\\iint_D 1\\,\\mathrm{d}A=$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/74/74735ff47608d314c82b66367404f6d2bb79c0c1e34b034083ebcd2d74dc55c0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $3$ centered at $(3,0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}=9$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-3)^{2}+y^{2}&=9\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-3)^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=9\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-6r\\cos\\theta+9+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=9\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-6r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-6\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/8b/8bafd3851f0378006c6e851b9bb60ab4bbcb1dadafffe4d5b5082d304ca7fed7.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/cc/ccdbd4ea144224d5793d4c48018147ca939cba611e5ea38ac649295218ca2f07.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D 1\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (1)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{6 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["980036b8-3088-5457-9263-9a3e9604d20c"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "96c17cb2-2129-5e02-9bdf-4663faa9470d" :answer/id #uuid "96c17cb2-2129-5e02-9bdf-4663faa9470d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3}drdθ" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "e5698084-b769-5ac6-b65b-adb1e670787d" :answer/id #uuid "e5698084-b769-5ac6-b65b-adb1e670787d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{\\frac{3}{2}}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4acdd0df-c785-59f2-9658-4e3a960a5cfe" :answer/id #uuid "4acdd0df-c785-59f2-9658-4e3a960a5cfe" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{2π}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{3}drdθ" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "a19c980b-b72c-5250-b5c3-5c4d8d144b94" :answer/id #uuid "a19c980b-b72c-5250-b5c3-5c4d8d144b94" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{3}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "5031c2db-393e-5fdd-8e60-3997b7fff3b2" :answer/id #uuid "5031c2db-393e-5fdd-8e60-3997b7fff3b2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "\\displaystyle \\int_{- π/2}^{π/2}\\displaystyle \\int_{0}^{3\\cos θ}r^{2}\\cos 2θdrdθ" :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "aafcfd12-6869-527e-91e6-ff53db154e0f" :answer-field/id #uuid "aafcfd12-6869-527e-91e6-ff53db154e0f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["96c17cb2-2129-5e02-9bdf-4663faa9470d" "e5698084-b769-5ac6-b65b-adb1e670787d" "4acdd0df-c785-59f2-9658-4e3a960a5cfe" "a19c980b-b72c-5250-b5c3-5c4d8d144b94" "5031c2db-393e-5fdd-8e60-3997b7fff3b2"] :answer-field/correct "a19c980b-b72c-5250-b5c3-5c4d8d144b94" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "d3a13c3d-8749-55b1-8f45-ee637a70eea2" :question/id #uuid "d3a13c3d-8749-55b1-8f45-ee637a70eea2" :question/problem "If $D = \\{(x, y): (x - \\frac{3}{2})^{2} + y^{2} \\le \\frac{9}{4}\\}$, then $∬_(D)(x^{2} - y^{2})dA =$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n\\iint_D f(x,y)\\,dA=\\iint_\\Delta f(r\\cos\\theta,r\\sin\\theta)\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/d0/d01daaf1f81b8dd2b2ab8d01bbbe5a7dd1f4262eb7de9d3977b8c1e104a90ec0.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(\\frac{3}{2},0).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\cos\\theta \\\\ y=r\\sin\\theta\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the $(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}+y^{2}&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\(r\\cos\\theta-\\frac{3}{2})^2+(r\\sin\\theta)^2&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2\\cos^2\\theta-3r\\cos\\theta+\\frac{9}{4}+r^2\\sin^2\\theta&=\\frac{9}{4}\\\\r^2(\\cos^2\\theta+\\sin^2\\theta)-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r^2-3r\\cos\\theta&=0\\\\r(r-3\\cos\\theta)&=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}.$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta.$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}$ (at the boundary of the region):\n\n![](images/ac/ac0374d74edf7f4490f7c160b929b9dc5d03d1324d7cb79dc691a5ca21248e35.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta$ can vary between $\\theta=- \\frac{\\pi}{2}$ and $\\theta=\\frac{\\pi}{2}.$\n\n![](images/c6/c674039d133057398ff5fd98770479e9fba7866524ead09c78adba2c2ac5cb80.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta),$ the region can be expressed as\n\n$$\n\\Delta=\\{(r,\\theta)\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)},\\,- \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\le\\theta\\le \\frac{\\pi}{2}\\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\iint_D x^{2} - y^{2}\\,dA&=\\iint_\\Delta (- r^{2} \\sin^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)} + r^{2} \\cos^{2}{\\left(\\theta \\right)})\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^3(\\cos^2\\theta-\\sin^2\\theta)\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta\\\\&=\\int_{- \\frac{\\pi}{2}}^{\\frac{\\pi}{2}}\\int_{0}^{3 \\cos{\\left(\\theta \\right)}}r^{3} \\cos{\\left(2 \\theta \\right)}\\,\\mathrm{d}r\\,\\mathrm{d}\\theta.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["aafcfd12-6869-527e-91e6-ff53db154e0f"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "63130042-5901-5034-86fc-435727d0f3f5" :answer/id #uuid "63130042-5901-5034-86fc-435727d0f3f5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "4cde6b07-0e2a-568b-abd2-30c5e1ac404b" :answer/id #uuid "4cde6b07-0e2a-568b-abd2-30c5e1ac404b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "f0fe3285-6e13-5d53-953e-91a811e1db98" :answer/id #uuid "f0fe3285-6e13-5d53-953e-91a811e1db98" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "7ee5d5f6-e473-541e-87b4-e95658ad2cff" :answer/id #uuid "7ee5d5f6-e473-541e-87b4-e95658ad2cff" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "010f0bb3-cfce-57dc-a3dd-0e6bf16e313e" :answer/id #uuid "010f0bb3-cfce-57dc-a3dd-0e6bf16e313e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "{\\int }_{0}^{2\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta " :db/ensure :answer/validate} {:db/id "3d84f478-b5b5-5f25-9156-e2d90cf503dc" :answer-field/id #uuid "3d84f478-b5b5-5f25-9156-e2d90cf503dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/choices ["63130042-5901-5034-86fc-435727d0f3f5" "4cde6b07-0e2a-568b-abd2-30c5e1ac404b" "f0fe3285-6e13-5d53-953e-91a811e1db98" "7ee5d5f6-e473-541e-87b4-e95658ad2cff" "010f0bb3-cfce-57dc-a3dd-0e6bf16e313e"] :answer-field/correct "4cde6b07-0e2a-568b-abd2-30c5e1ac404b" :db/ensure :answer-field/validate} {:db/id "dc21b297-d346-5eac-82b2-8f472263113f" :question/id #uuid "dc21b297-d346-5eac-82b2-8f472263113f" :question/problem "If $D=\\{(x,y)\\,:\\,{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}\\le \\frac{9}{4}\\},$ then ${∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A=$\n\nChoose the polar setup whose bounds describe $D$ with $r\\ge 0$ and whose integrand is the given Cartesian integrand multiplied by the Jacobian $r.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to change the coordinate system from Cartesian coordinates to polar coordinates. To do this, we will use the change of variables formula in the form\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}f(x,y)\\,dA={∬}_{Δ}\\,f(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )\\,r\\,dr\\,d\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, let's sketch our region $D:$\n\n![](images/07/074eb42aff2c78d93136fc9e56b2420450f72c5d34c6b12da8bf84e080db75ea.png)\n\nNotice that the region $D$ represents the disk of radius $\\frac{3}{2}$ centered at $(0,\\frac{3}{2}).$\n\nCartesian and polar coordinates are connected by the following equations:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\ y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's now write the ${x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2}=\\frac{9}{4}$ in polar coordinates:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}+(y-\\frac{3}{2})^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {(r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta )}^{2}+{(r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\frac{3}{2})}^{2} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{r}^{2}{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta -3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\frac{9}{4} & =\\frac{9}{4} \\\\ {r}^{2}({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ {r}^{2}-3r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  & =0 \\\\ r(r-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ) & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSince $r$ cannot be identical to zero on the entire circle, we must have $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .$\n\nNow, let's determine the limits for $r$ and $\\theta .$ First of all, $r$ can vary from $r=0$ (at the origin) to $r=3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta $ (at the boundary of the region): \n\n![](images/ce/cea2b2ddd6b211073b579d71bd8ed08405fa04e354825a124acb71bedf413295.png)\n\nSecondly, $\\theta $ can vary between $\\theta =0$ (the positive $x$-axis) and $\\theta =\\pi $ (the negative $x$-axis):\n\n![](images/bb/bbe07b71b3c82cde2e9175b06904905206984b04ca9a8ccd746b051212f5e8d8.png)\n\nSo, in polar coordinates $(r,\\theta ),$ the region can be expressed as \n\n$$\nΔ=\\{(r,\\theta )\\,:\\,0\\le r\\le 3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ,\\,0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi \\}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, using the change of variables formula, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∬}_{D}x\\,\\text{d}A & ={∬}_{Δ}r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\cdot r\\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta  \\\\  & ={\\int }_{0}^{\\pi }{\\int }_{0}^{3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{r}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,\\text{d}r\\,\\text{d}\\theta .\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["3d84f478-b5b5-5f25-9156-e2d90cf503dc"] :db/ensure :question/validate} {:db/id "generator" :question-generator/id #uuid "39cf5ac3-e106-5836-a2d9-4f3d2c075754" :question-generator/script "generate/scratch/17592186129275.py"} [:db/add 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/question-generator "generator"] {:db/id 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/questions ["7d4f7134-3d22-5cbc-8e83-54b913ada494" "9021eafc-a91a-5e03-85b9-0baff1b49b29" "06448e69-2e5e-5172-aee5-b3151a22cc6e" "fa53fde6-b454-5423-91b4-30fdb6c5d5b2" "b2b48fc3-a0dd-5bed-af7d-8c8aed9c00ae" "d3a13c3d-8749-55b1-8f45-ee637a70eea2" "dc21b297-d346-5eac-82b2-8f472263113f"]} [:db/ensure 17592186129275 :knowledge-point/validate]]
